470 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 232 0 R/E(Angeleno Corps is a 10-month program that provides 18- to 30-year-olds with the opportunity to gain work experience through service learning, pursue their education, and receive a $1,000 monthly stipend. 350 participants complete their service at a community-based organization in one of six areas: Education and Child Care; Health, Climate, and Environmental Justice; Immigration Assistance; Nutrition and Food Justice; Technology and Digital Assistance; and Housing Rights Advocacy. 50+ community-based organizations will host Corps members and provide direct work experience and supervision at their worksites. )/Pg 18 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

471 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 232 0 R/E(The Automotive and Warehouse Toolroom Worker Apprenticeship/Internships provide 148 hours of on-the job training to 10 high school or college students, ages 16-30. Created as a potential pathway opportunity into City employment, students participate in a 9-week internship with the City\220s General Service Department and, if successful, will receive a certificate of completion that details the competencies acquired at the end of the program.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

472 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 232 0 R/E(Clean L.A. is a 12-month, full-time employment program for young adults between the ages of 18-30 to help clean and green the City of Los Angeles as a pathway to a full-time career. The program focuses on employing job-seeking Angelenos to perform community beautification and street clean-up services in areas of the city with the greatest need. Work responsibilities include, but are not limited to: weed abatement, cleaning/removing loose litter, and clearing bulky items from the public right-of-way. )/Pg 18 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

473 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 232 0 R/E(The City Pathways for Youth program provides young people between the ages of 16-30 years old with the opportunity to learn about careers in the City of Los Angeles\220 Recreation and Parks Department. Interns are placed at recreation centers and park sites across the city, working alongside recreational and maintenance professionals to create a welcoming environment for the local community. They receive hands-on training and supervision to support afterschool programs, sports/recreation programs, and other community engagement activities.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

474 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 232 0 R/E(Digital Ambassadors are HACLA resident leaders between the ages of 18-30 who support their communities in addressing the digital divide. Participants are paid $20.00 an hour to assist low-income and senior households with acquiring low-cost internet or digital devices and navigating online resources such as virtual learning, employment opportunities, and tele-health services. )/Pg 18 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

475 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 232 0 R/E(The Early Childhood Education Student Advancement Program is an internship and mentorship program for students enrolled in a post-secondary educational institution majoring in the child development \(or closely related\) field. Students will receive training in child behavior and psychology, communication, and problem-solving, along with one-on-one mentorship support and leadership development. Students can also participate in the ECE Co-generational Connection Program \(ECE-CCPro\) that connects them with mentors using a Sesame Street-inspired curriculum. In partnership with the Child Development Consortium of Los Angeles \(CCDLA\), students are placed in paid interships to receive practical, hands-on experience with infants and toddlers at child care sites.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

476 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 232 0 R/Pg 18 0 R/A[1113 0 R 0]/K[8]>>
endobj

