1520 0 obj
<</Title(Brenda Shockley)/Dest[11 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1317 0 R/Prev 1519 0 R/Next 1507 0 R>>
endobj

1521 0 obj
<</Title(In Los Angeles, young people from low-income households, foster care backgrounds, or justice-impacted communities often face barriers to entering the workforce, including limited access to paid experience, professional networks, and clear pathways into stable careers. YSC-LA addresses these gaps by positioning employment as both an economic intervention and a workforce development strategy, ensuring that opportunity is aligned with community need and future labor demand.)/Dest[12 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1487 0 R/Prev 1509 0 R/Next 1510 0 R>>
endobj

1522 0 obj
<</Title(YSC-LA is implemented through a coordinated partnership involving the Mayor\220s Office of Economic Opportunity and key City departments, supported by a $22 million statewide investment through California Volunteers Office of the Governor. This investment strengthens Los Angeles\220 youth workforce infrastructure, enabling cross-department collaboration, expanded placements, and enhanced case management capacity.)/Dest[12 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1510 0 R>>
endobj

1523 0 obj
<</Title(YSC-LA is implemented through a coordinated citywide partnership led by the Mayor\220s Office of Economic Opportunity in collaboration with:)/Dest[13 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1489 0 R/Prev 1511 0 R/Next 1533 0 R>>
endobj

1524 0 obj
<</Title(The initiative operates within Los Angeles\220 broader youth workforce development ecosystem, working closely with YouthSource, FamilySource, and WorkSource Centers; the Los Angeles Unified School District \(LAUSD\); the Los Angeles Community College District \(LACCD\) and a consortium of experienced nonprofit service providers that serve as worksites.)/Dest[13 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1489 0 R/Prev 1534 0 R/Next 1512 0 R>>
endobj

1525 0 obj
<</Title(The demand for structured youth employment in Los Angeles continues to outpace available placements. This year, nearly 8,000 applications were submitted for YSC-LA opportunities, underscoring both the need for paid work experience and the strong desire among young Angelenos to contribute meaningfully to their communities. This level of interest highlights the importance of maintaining and expanding access to high-quality workforce pathways across the City.)/Dest[13 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1513 0 R/Next 1526 0 R>>
endobj

1526 0 obj
<</Title(The scale of participation and level of investment reflect the City\220s commitment to aligning opportunity with community need and advancing workforce equity at scale.)/Dest[13 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1513 0 R/Prev 1525 0 R>>
endobj

1527 0 obj
<</Title(Ongoing Committment)/Dest[13 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 1513 0 R/First 1535 0 R/Last 1536 0 R/Count -2/Next 1537 0 R>>
endobj

1528 0 obj
<</Title(L.A. River Rangers)/Dest[16 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 1538 0 R/Next 1514 0 R>>
endobj

1529 0 obj
<</Title(In 2025, the Department of Recreation and Parks continued to shine as a leader in Youth Service Corps, stewarding two impactful programs that are opening real doors for young Angelenos. Through City Pathways to Childcare and City Pathways for Youth, RAP not only provided meaningful work experience, but helped participants see long-term careers in public service and parks as a viable and exciting path forward.)/Dest[16 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1514 0 R/First 1539 0 R/Last 1539 0 R/Count -1/Next 1530 0 R>>
endobj

1530 0 obj
<</Title(MAY 2026)/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1514 0 R/Prev 1529 0 R>>
endobj

1531 0 obj
<</Title(Student to Student Success \(S2S\) provides high school students with paid work experience while they support elementary students in after-school settings. Participants are placed at LA\220s BEST \(Better Educated Students for Tomorrow\), a City of Los Angeles\205affiliated after-school program, where they assist with academic and enrichment activities for younger students.)/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1516 0 R/Next 1532 0 R>>
endobj

1532 0 obj
<</Title(Through this partnership, participants can transition the following semester into Part-Time Program Helper roles with LA\220s BEST, increasing their pay, gaining more flexible hours, and becoming LA\220s BEST employees\204strengthening both youth workforce pathways and support for elementary students across the City\220s after-school programs.)/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1516 0 R/Prev 1531 0 R>>
endobj

1533 0 obj
<</Title(The Mayor\220s Office of Community Safety)/Dest[13 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1489 0 R/Prev 1523 0 R/Next 1540 0 R>>
endobj

1534 0 obj
<</Title(Together, these entities work alongside the City\220s contracted YouthSource Centers and a broad network of community-based organizations to recruit, enroll, and support participants.)/Dest[13 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1489 0 R/Prev 1541 0 R/Next 1524 0 R>>
endobj

1535 0 obj
<</Title(Across all projects, the overarching goal remains clear: connect young job seekers to high-quality workforce programs that enable them not only to secure their first job, but to maintain stable employment and access long-term careers in public and community service, increasing their lifetime earning potential. To ensure continuous improvement, the Mayor\220s Office of Economic Opportunity tracks enrollment, completion, and participant outcomes, maintaining a data-driven approach that responds to the evolving needs of youth and the local labor market.)/Dest[13 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1527 0 R/Next 1536 0 R>>
endobj

1536 0 obj
<</Title(MAY 2026)/Dest[14 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1527 0 R/Prev 1535 0 R>>
endobj

