endobj

1538 0 obj
<</Title(131)/Dest[15 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 1545 0 R/First 1546 0 R/Last 1547 0 R/Count -2/Next 1528 0 R>>
endobj

1539 0 obj
<</Title(This year, City Pathways to Childcare supported 7 interns, with 6 transitioning into part-time employment at RAP or partner agencies after completing their internship. Meanwhile, City Pathways for Youth engaged 82 interns across parks citywide, with 20 moving directly into employment with RAP. These outcomes reflect more than placements; they represent a growing pipeline into careers in recreation, childcare, and community-based public service.)/Dest[16 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1529 0 R>>
endobj

1540 0 obj
<</Title(The Economic and Workforce Development Department \(EWDD\))/Dest[13 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1489 0 R/Prev 1533 0 R/Next 1548 0 R>>
endobj

1541 0 obj
<</Title(The Housing Authority of the City of Los Angeles \(HACLA\))/Dest[13 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1489 0 R/Prev 1549 0 R/Next 1534 0 R>>
endobj

1542 0 obj
<</Title(Youth Service Corps Los Angeles \(YSC-LA\) operated 16 programs across the City, including one high school\205focused initiative. While age eligibility ranged from 16 to 30, the majority of programs served young adults preparing to enter or transition within the workforce.)/Dest[14 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1537 0 R/Next 1550 0 R>>
endobj

1543 0 obj
<</Title(Taken together, these metrics demonstrate more than program volume. They reflect a workforce model that is reaching young people with the greatest barriers to employment, converting demand into structured opportunity, and translating public investment into measurable service hours within local communities. As YSC-LA continues to evolve, these data points provide a foundation for targeted growth, expanded regional access, and strengthened long-term workforce pathways.)/Dest[14 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1537 0 R/Prev 1551 0 R/First 1552 0 R/Last 1553 0 R/Count -2>>
endobj

