<</Title(Behind the data are programs delivering tangible impact across Los Angeles. The videos below highlight how Youth Service Corps Los Angeles \(YSC-LA\) participants are strengthening communities through environmental stewardship and public service while building career pathways of their own.)/Dest[16 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1547 0 R/Next 1564 0 R>>
endobj

1559 0 obj
<</Title(The team also supported fire season vegetation management, led volunteer cleanups in the Sepulveda Basin, and installed native plants near the LAX dunes. A total of 72 youth participated in the program, with 32 transitioning into employment after service, reinforcing the River Rangers as both an environmental force and a strong pathway into climate and public works careers.)/Dest[16 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1547 0 R/Prev 1564 0 R>>
endobj

1560 0 obj
<</Title(The Department of Recreation and Parks \(RAP\))/Dest[13 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1489 0 R/Prev 1548 0 R/Next 1549 0 R>>
endobj

1561 0 obj
<</Title(Of the youth placed, 1,280 completed 100 percent of their expected service hours. Program models ranged from 140 hours to 1,900 hours depending on the initiative. Based on completed service to date, participating youth worked an estimated 368,583 hours in communities across Los Angeles and earned approximately $9.4 million in wages and stipends. Final accounting is ongoing.)/Dest[14 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1537 0 R/Prev 1550 0 R/Next 1562 0 R>>
endobj

1562 0 obj
<</Title(All 2,244 youth placed met low-income eligibility requirements. In addition:)/Dest[14 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1537 0 R/Prev 1561 0 R/Next 1551 0 R>>
